    "result_text": "Tests are green. Both findings were valid and are now addressed.\n\n## Changes made\n\n**Finding 1 \u2014 household conflict funneled through the wrong exception type (agreed, fixed).**\nA household duplicate is conceptually distinct from an identity-key collision, and this codebase's idiom is one exception per conflict kind (`OwnerCityAtCapacityException`, etc.). I:\n- Added `DuplicateHouseholdException` carrying the `householdId`, with its own message (`\"An owner in household <id> already exists\"`).\n- Added a dedicated advice handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice` with its own `ERROR_DUPLICATE_HOUSEHOLD` detail, a `\"Duplicate household\"` log line, and `errors: [\"householdId\"]` \u2014 so a household-duplicate rejection no longer masquerades as an identity-key collision in logs or in the client response.\n- Changed `rejectHouseholdDuplicate` to throw the new type (and updated its `@throws`).\n\nThe `DuplicateOwnerIdentityException` is now back to meaning strictly an identity-key collision, matching its Javadoc and `getIdentityKey()`.\n\n**Finding 2 \u2014 stale `normalizeAddress` Javadoc (agreed, fixed).**\nRemoved the clause claiming the normalized address is \"the form every address comparison (household duplicate detection and the shared household id) uses,\" since address no longer feeds either \u2014 both now derive from last name + postcode. The doc now just states the normalized value is stored and returned.\n\nBoth HTTP conflicts remain 409 with identical acceptance behaviour (the `sharesHousehold` bypass path is unchanged), and the test suite passes (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`).",
